XOR removes every pair because `x ^ x` is zero. After XORing the whole array, the remaining value is `a ^ b`, where `a` and `b` are the two answers.

A set bit in `a ^ b` means that `a` and `b` differ at that bit. Use the rightmost such bit to split the numbers into two groups. Each pair stays in one group, while the two single values go into different groups.

For `[1, 2, 1, 3, 2, 5]`, the total XOR is `6`. Its rightmost set bit separates `3` and `5`, and XORing each group returns those values.

```go
func singleNumber(nums []int) []int {
	xorAll := 0
	for _, value := range nums {
		xorAll ^= value
	}

	differentBit := xorAll & -xorAll
	first := 0
	second := 0

	for _, value := range nums {
		if value&differentBit != 0 {
			first ^= value
		} else {
			second ^= value
		}
	}

	return []int{first, second}
}
```

If the two unique values differ only in the sign bit, `differentBit` still works with Go's signed integer bit operations.

Time complexity: `O(n)` with two passes through the slice.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

Sorting puts equal values beside each other, so a pair can be skipped together. Any value that is not equal to its neighbor is one of the answers.

This changes the input vector by sorting it. That is the main trade-off compared with a hash map or XOR.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> singleNumber(vector<int>& nums) {
        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());
        vector<int> result;
        int i = 0;

        while (i + 1 < static_cast<int>(nums.size())) {
            if (nums[i] == nums[i + 1]) {
                i += 2;
            } else {
                result.push_back(nums[i]);
                ++i;
            }
        }

        if (i < static_cast<int>(nums.size())) {
            result.push_back(nums[i]);
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

Dry run with `[4, 1, 2, 1, 2, 7]`: after sorting, the array is `[1, 1, 2, 2, 4, 7]`. The pairs are skipped, leaving `4` and `7`.

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` because of sorting.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space, not counting the sorting implementation.

A set is enough when every repeated number appears exactly twice. The first copy is inserted. The second copy removes it. At the end, only the two values that appeared once remain.

The result order comes from the set and is not important.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int[] SingleNumber(int[] nums) {
        HashSet<int> values = new HashSet<int>();

        foreach (int value in nums) {
            if (values.Contains(value)) {
                values.Remove(value);
            } else {
                values.Add(value);
            }
        }

        int[] result = new int[2];
        int index = 0;
        foreach (int value in values) {
            result[index++] = value;
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

The important edge case is when a unique value is negative or zero. The set treats those values just like any other integer, so no special check is needed.

Time complexity: `O(n)` average time.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the set.

This is the direct version. For each number, scan the rest of the array to see whether another copy exists. If no copy is found, add that number to the answer and stop after finding two values.

For `nums = [1, 2, 1, 3, 2, 5]`, the checks skip `1` and `2`, then keep `3` and `5`.

```python
class Solution:
    def singleNumber(self, nums):
        result = []
        n = len(nums)

        for i in range(n):
            has_pair = False
            for j in range(n):
                if i != j and nums[i] == nums[j]:
                    has_pair = True
                    break

            if not has_pair:
                result.append(nums[i])
                if len(result) == 2:
                    break

        return result
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the two-value result list.

An input with only two elements is handled as well, since neither element can have a second copy.

A frequency map keeps this version easy to follow. Count every value first, then collect the two values whose count is one. The order of the returned values does not matter.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] singleNumber(int[] nums) {
        java.util.HashMap<Integer, Integer> counts = new java.util.HashMap<>();

        for (int value : nums) {
            counts.put(value, counts.getOrDefault(value, 0) + 1);
        }

        int[] result = new int[2];
        int index = 0;
        for (java.util.Map.Entry<Integer, Integer> entry : counts.entrySet()) {
            if (entry.getValue() == 1) {
                result[index++] = entry.getKey();
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the map.

